   "source": [
    "# ✍️ Building a CNN That Reads Handwritten Digits\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook we will build a **Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)** that looks at a picture of a handwritten digit (0–9) and tells us which digit it is.\n",
    "\n",
    "We do it in three parts:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Part | What we do |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Part 1** | Train a CNN on **MNIST**, a famous dataset of 70,000 handwritten digits |\n",
    "| **Part 2** | Test the model on **our own photo** of a digit, cleaning the photo up with **OpenCV** |\n",
    "| **Part 3** | Do the same cleanup again using **Pillow (PIL)** instead of OpenCV |\n",
    "\n",
    "### How to use this notebook\n",
    "- Run the cells **from top to bottom, in order**. Later cells depend on variables created by earlier ones.\n",
    "- Read the text above each cell *before* you run it, then read the comments (lines starting with `#`) inside the cell.\n",
    "- After a cell runs, compare the output to the **\"What you should see\"** note.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The big idea (read this first!)\n",
    "A neural network can only learn what it was shown. The model in Part 1 is trained on MNIST images: **white digits on a black background, 28 × 28 pixels, centered, thick strokes.** A photo from your phone looks nothing like that: it is large, colored, has shadows, and the digit is dark ink on light paper. So most of Parts 2 and 3 is about one job: **making our photo look like an MNIST image.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e635a285",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# Part 1: Training the CNN on MNIST\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 1: Import the libraries\n",
    "\n",
    "A **library** is a collection of ready-made code we can reuse instead of writing everything from scratch.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Library | What we use it for |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| `numpy` | Storing and calculating with numbers. **Images are just grids of numbers**, so NumPy is the backbone of this notebook. |\n",
    "| `matplotlib` | Drawing images and graphs |\n",
    "| `tensorflow.keras` | Building and training neural networks |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "208433b7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# NumPy: fast arrays and maths. We nickname it \"np\" so we can type less.\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "# Matplotlib's plotting tool. We nickname it \"plt\".\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "# Sequential: a model made of layers stacked one after another, like a sandwich.\n",
    "from tensorflow.keras import Sequential\n",
    "\n",
    "# The layer types (the \"ingredients\") we will use in our model:\n",
    "from tensorflow.keras.layers import (\n",
    "    Input,         # tells the model what shape of data to expect\n",
    "    Conv2D,        # convolution layer: finds patterns such as edges and curves\n",
    "    MaxPooling2D,  # shrinks the image while keeping the strongest signals\n",
    "    Flatten,       # turns a 2D grid of features into one long list\n",
    "    Dense,         # a normal fully-connected layer that makes decisions\n",
    "    Dropout        # randomly switches off neurons during training to prevent over-fitting\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# MNIST: the dataset of handwritten digits that ships with Keras\n",
    "from tensorflow.keras.datasets import mnist"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8232f2c6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2: Load the MNIST dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "MNIST contains **70,000** grayscale images of handwritten digits, already split for us:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Training set**: 60,000 images the model *learns from*\n",
    "- **Test set**: 10,000 images the model has *never seen*, used to check if it really learned\n",
    "\n",
    "Each image comes with a **label**, the correct answer (0–9). The naming convention is:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `X` = the inputs (images)\n",
    "- `Y` = the answers (labels)\n",
    "\n",
    "**What you should see:** `(60000, 28, 28)` means 60,000 images, each 28 pixels tall and 28 pixels wide. Labels are just one number per image, so their shape is `(60000,)`."
   ]
  },
  {
